              {
                "text": "**Analyzing the Transformation**\n\nI'm currently focused on unraveling the underlying transformation rule. I've begun by examining training examples, particularly the input-output pairs to discern the pattern. Specifically, I'm trying to decipher the relationship between the input dimensions and the output dimensions, considering potential tiling or block repetition approaches. I think the output matrix is twice the height and width of the input.\n\n\n**Deciphering the Projection**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. It seems values \"project\" vertically, with intermediate cells becoming 8. Positive indices project downwards, negative indices project upwards. I'm focusing on why a 0 at a position in the input matrix always becomes an 8, and other values project downwards or upwards. The output matrix is a 2x2 tiling of the transformed block. I think I'm seeing a general pattern, but I need to test it thoroughly.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Projection**\n\nI've been examining how individual pixels are projected. I now believe values project vertically based on their row position. If a pixel is at the top row, it projects downwards; if at the bottom, it projects upwards. I'm currently focused on how pixels project from the middle rows of an input matrix, and how that corresponds to the output.\n\n\n**Unveiling Pixel Connections**\n\nI've made a crucial observation. The 2x2 tiling of the grid repeats all pixels, but not directly. I've realized that the 8s are \"between\" values that are repeated. I'm focusing on why an 8 appears between the original value and its copy in the tiled grid. This reveals a connection between the rows in the matrix.\n\n\n**Confirming the Pattern**\n\nI've made a significant breakthrough! My focus has shifted to the columns and I've confirmed a key aspect of the transformation. I've realized that every column in the input matrix with at least one non-zero value has all its zeros replaced by eights in the output matrix. I believe this explains many of the observed tiling behaviors, and I am now testing this hypothesis on every example.\n\n\n**Validating the Hypothesis**\n\nI've just validated the final hypothesis, and I'm very excited because the process is complete! The transformation rule is elegant and straightforward: any column with at least one non-zero entry replaces all zeros in that column with eights, and then the resulting grid is tiled 2x2. I've now confirmed the rule on all of the training examples. It seems to work consistently. I'm excited to test it on the next inputs.\n\n\n",
